==============================================================================
READING
==============================================================================

The distinct-section count is a product over regions. If each of six regions
holds its pick with probability p independently, the whole section reproduces
with probability p^6: p = 0.95 gives 0.74, p = 0.80 gives 0.26. So a 26.6%
modal rate is what ~80% per-region stability looks like, and the statistic
compounds region count into what reads as a statement about the table.

Regret is the statistic that says whether a flip matters. A region where two
arms score within a fraction of a percent flips constantly and costs that
fraction of a percent when it flips.

The control arm is the load-bearing comparison. `bitpack-carrier-d16-control`
is byte-identical to `bitpack-carrier-d16` by construction and by the bench's
own documented intent, so every flip between those two is the harness's
declared noise floor appearing inside an argmin. Compare the two arm sets
above: what survives when it is dropped is the instability that is about the
table rather than about the instrument.

None of this is a bench and no measurement was taken.

